WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.013 --> 00:00:01.430
JENNY BROWN: Our
last video talked

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:03.260
about some major
configuration options

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:06.257
when using the Cloud Talent
Solution Job Search API.

00:00:06.257 --> 00:00:08.090
There are some simpler
configuration options

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:10.910
that we have recommendations
for and some best practices

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.090
when testing out
your API integration.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.840
Let's start with the
configuration options

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.940
when executing a search
request against the API.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.860
First up, page size is
a pretty simple one.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.890
It's number of jobs that get
returned in a single response.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.140
We typically recommend
sticking to 20 or fewer

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.430
to keep things quick.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.013
Now, if you're dealing with jobs
in multiple geographic areas,

00:00:34.013 --> 00:00:36.013
you'll want to make sure
that you set the region

00:00:36.013 --> 00:00:38.150
code through location
filters and language code

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:39.440
for each search.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.410
The search's region code will
match the jobs region code only

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.460
to display results
in the right regions.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.790
For example, if a
region code isn't set,

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:49.070
a search for Cambridge
might return job results

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.340
from Cambridge in the UK
and Cambridge in the US.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.340
The language code will help the
API localize the search query

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.930
to increase the
relevance of results

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.763
through language-specific
terminology.

00:00:58.763 --> 00:01:00.680
Not only can you set the
region for searching,

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.780
you can also set a
distance in miles.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.750
Setting the distance to 50 would
limit the search results return

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.050
to only be within 50 miles of
the user's indicated address

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:10.460
or lat long.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.570
Like most of the
configuration options,

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.140
you should tweak this to what
works best for your use case

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.450
or expose an option to the user.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:19.937
The fewer restrictions you
place on the query results,

00:01:19.937 --> 00:01:21.770
the more results you'll
get, but potentially

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:22.880
with less relevancy.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.830
There are also some
configuration options

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:27.620
you can set on individual
job postings to help the API

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.530
when searching, such as the
location we just mentioned.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:31.990
When setting the
location of a job,

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:33.740
you should provide the
address of the job,

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.920
if possible, rather
than the GPS coordinate.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.960
We'll go over more
details in future videos

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.150
when we talk about
creating jobs in detail,

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.250
but the API uses
addresses to help

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:46.160
with location detection
and accurate results.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.050
In general, making
sure jobs have

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:50.330
locations helps users
have a better experience

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:51.410
when searching.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.230
For each job, you can also
set an expiration time.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.630
After that time, the job no
longer appears in results

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.040
when performing a search.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.870
By default, the
expiration time is

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:03.290
30 days after the job is
created in the UTC time zone,

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:06.360
but you can change the value
for each job as necessary.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.240
Again, we recommend
making sure to set

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.520
this property to what makes
the most sense for you

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.660
and your users.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:13.460
There is also an
employment type to help

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.130
make sure users have more
choice about what types of jobs

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.150
are returned.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.240
Typical values can include
full time or part time,

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.460
but there are a number of
options to help narrow down

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:24.590
what can be returned,
and not all options

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.240
may be applicable to you.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.000
Finally, there's the
custom attributes property.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.400
This allows you to add
additional values to each job

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.680
so that you can filter
by them as needed,

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.990
or to use with custom
ranking, like we talked about

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.290
in the last video.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.650
For example, you may
want to filter by GPA

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.450
required or industry type.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:44.180
Now, we don't recommend setting
up any custom attributes

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.040
for location-based properties.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.070
That's because the API already
has location logic specific

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:51.530
to the location field on
a job that we've already

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:52.940
touched a bit on.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.710
With those configuration
options in mind,

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.377
you'll have a way to
better control what

00:02:56.377 --> 00:02:58.100
jobs get returned for a search.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:59.660
There are also
some best practices

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.790
when designing your
integration with the API that

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.140
are worth noting.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.260
Make sure you do your best to
avoid uploading duplicate jobs.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.880
While the API has
protection to make

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.790
sure nearly identical jobs
don't show up next each other,

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.260
it's still a poor
user experience

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.990
to see the same job
listing multiple times.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.540
When creating a job in
the API, it automatically

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:21.260
gets rejected if it's not unique
according to a few parameters.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.110
Two jobs can't share the
same combination of company,

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.330
requisition ID, and location.

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:29.330
Again, we'll look more at these
when we go over creating jobs,

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:32.000
but make sure to avoid any
job duplicates before trying

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.710
to create them in the API.

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:35.330
Speaking of errors,
you'll also want

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.455
to make sure you've set up
your integration to have

00:03:37.455 --> 00:03:38.840
appropriate error handling.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:40.910
When running any requests,
make sure to check

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.765
for the error codes in case
the request didn't go through.

00:03:43.765 --> 00:03:45.140
Some common errors
we've seen are

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:47.390
cases where the job
location is set incorrectly

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:51.232
when uploading a job or when
required fields aren't set.

00:03:51.232 --> 00:03:53.440
You can check out the full
list of all response codes

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:54.290
in the link below.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.240
When debugging or
investigating an error,

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:58.805
we recommend starting with
your local logging sources.

00:03:58.805 --> 00:04:01.430
You can then check out to Cloud
Talent Solution Management Tool

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.150
to see what may have happened.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.060
It's also possible
to setup Stackdriver

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.400
to collect all the metadata,
metrics, and events

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:08.690
that you'll need to debug.

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:10.760
If the error code is
related to quota or sending

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.205
too many requests, you may
need to check the Cloud Talent

00:04:13.205 --> 00:04:15.830
Solution Console, which you can
access through the Google Cloud

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.829
Console for any
indexing restrictions.

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:20.329
It's good to know if you'll be
limited on the number of jobs

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:23.340
you can index at any given
time before you upload any jobs

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:24.710
and to prepare accordingly.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:26.540
When handling an
actual error, we also

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:29.308
recommend doing exponential
back off for any retries.

00:04:29.308 --> 00:04:31.350
There's another link in
the description for this,

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:33.830
but the basic idea is to
ensure you design your system

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:36.380
in a way that doesn't just
spam the API with requests

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:37.270
after a failure.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:39.770
These are some high level things
to keep in mind as you look

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.500
to start integrating your jobs
and data with the job search

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:43.368
API.

00:04:43.368 --> 00:04:45.410
In our next video, we'll
walk through the details

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.060
of data that gets
sent to the API,

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.070
such as jobs and companies.

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:50.720
Thanks for watching,
and remember,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.210
when looking for talent, it's OK
to keep your head in the cloud.

